  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1... \nA strong cold front will move across the area this morning, \nallowing widespread rain to spread southeast across the local \narea early this morning. Rain may be moderate at times and most\nlocations will most likely receive a quarter to a half inch of \nrainfall by late morning. Flooding is not anticipated given the \nrecent stretch of minimal rainfall. Thunder potential is low \ngiven the lack of instability, but the dynamic nature of the \ncold front poses a slight chances of a few rumbles of thunder \nacross portions of NW OH Winds will be a bit gusty with the \nfrontal passage this morning with periodic gusts to 30 to 35 mph\nlikely along the immediate lakeshore early this morning. Gusts\nwill be lower inland, although it will still be breezy. Rain \nwill quickly exit the area by early to mid afternoon.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2... \nCanadian high pressure will quickly build southeast across the\nCWA behind the aforementioned cold front later today and \ngradually drift east across the region Saturday before exiting \nto the east by Sunday. A weak cold front will cross the area \nduring the day Sunday, although guidance favors a dry frontal \npassage. Behind this front, a stronger Canadian high will build \nover the region, delivering a dry and chillier air mass to the \narea for the first half of next week. Temperatures will be in \nthe mid-60s on Saturday, but warm air advection ahead of \nSunday's cold front will allow late weekend highs to reach the \nupper 60s and lower 70s. From there, highs on Monday and Tuesday\nwill only reach the low to mid 60s with many spots across \ninterior NW PA possibly staying in the 50s all day. Overnight \nlows will generally be in the 40s to around 50 degrees each \nnight, however efficient radiational cooling will result in \nwidespread lows in the upper 30s and lower 40s (with locally \nwarmer lows in the upper 40s along the lakeshore). Monday will \nmost likely be the cooler night of two and patchy to areas of \nfrost are possibly across interior locations and especially \ntypical cold spots. Will need to continue to monitor the \nforecast and would not be surprised if Frost Advisories ended \nup being issued for Monday night. High temperatures will warm \ninto the upper 60s and lower 70s Wednesday.",
